{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":279098,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"6a0f20c8-b62c-478a-908f-ac516dfdaefe","total_cost_usd":1.3316535,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":66864,"cache_read_input_tokens":20101,"output_tokens":24136,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":66864,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":24136,"cache_read_input_tokens":20101,"cache_creation_input_tokens":66864,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":66864},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":49453,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.049553,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":24136,"cacheReadInputTokens":20101,"cacheCreationInputTokens":66864,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.2821005,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"spec-auditor\",\n  \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n  \"summary\": \"Three failures, all in the loot table: the catalog's band_summary disagrees with the debris it summarises in five fields, the high band's fragile share is 1 in 2.3 rather than the spec's 1 in 4, and a full base hold adds 11% of ship mass where 2.3.5 demands roughly 100% — every pricing, ablation, landing, tow-fee, EVA, staging and upgrade rule in the params holds.\",\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_names_consistent\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band name used in the catalog or the params appears in the baseline's bands, and no band is referenced that the baseline does not define.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Baseline defines {suborbital, low, high}. Catalog uses exactly those three across all 30 debris entries and all 3 band_summary entries. Params reference them in ablation.heat_index, ablation.cost_curve, ablation.optimal_skims and economy.band_value_multiplier — 4 objects x 3 keys, all matching, no fourth name anywhere.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_summary_matches_catalog\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band_summary entry agrees with the debris array it summarises: piece_count, mean_mass_kg and fragile_fraction all recomputed.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"piece_count is correct in all three bands (9 / 10 / 11 entries counted). mean_mass_kg: suborbital sum 9+31+4+48+6.5+7+40+13+210 = 368.5 kg / 9 = 40.94, stated 36.5 (12.2% low); low sum 685.5 / 10 = 68.55, stated 68.55 (agrees); high sum 130+96+78+64+21+150+112+33+47+124+520 = 1375 / 11 = 125.00, stated 98.64 (26.7% low). fragile_fraction (spawn-weighted, the convention the suborbital figure confirms): suborbital 13/105 = 0.1238, stated 0.1238 (agrees); low 22/97 = 0.2268, stated 0.2062; high 41/96 = 0.4271, stated 0.4038. Five of nine recomputed fields disagree with the array.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Recompute mean_mass_kg for suborbital and high and fragile_fraction for low and high directly from the debris array, or correct whichever piece masses and spawn_weights drifted after the summary was written.\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"mean_mass_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"The catalog's mean piece mass rises band over band, so valuable junk physically fights you.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Recomputed simple means 40.94 -> 68.55 -> 125.00 kg (x1.67 then x1.82, x3.05 end to end). Excluding the crane-only oversized pieces, the hand-tetherable means are 158.5/8 = 19.81 -> 345.5/9 = 38.39 -> 855/10 = 85.50 kg, a x4.32 climb suborbital to high. Monotone increasing on both readings.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_share_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile spawn share rises across the bands, roughly 1 in 10 suborbital to 1 in 4 high.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Recomputed spawn-weighted fragile share: suborbital 13/105 = 0.1238 (1 in 8.1, close enough to 1 in 10), low 22/97 = 0.2268 (1 in 4.4), high 41/96 = 0.4271 (1 in 2.3). The share does rise monotonically, but the high band is 71% above the spec's 1-in-4 target (0.4271 vs 0.25) — five of its eleven types are fragile, so nearly half of top-band spawns can never be crushed at any compactor tier.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Lower the high band's fragile spawn_weights (the smallest-value fragile types, ion_thruster_grid at 7 and atomic_clock_package at 6, are the cheapest cuts) until the weighted share lands near 0.25.\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"full_hold_doubles_mass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"A full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass at starting storage size.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"base_slots 6 against dry_mass_kg 900. Representative slice mass per crushed slot from the catalog: suborbital and low hand-tetherable pieces average 504/17 = 29.6 kg per piece at roughly 1.8 slots per piece, i.e. ~16 kg per slot — the simulator measures 16.23 kg/slot and full_hold_kg 97.4 independently. 6 x 16.23 = 97.4 kg on 900 kg = 1.11x, against a required ~2.0x (tolerance 1.5x-2.5x). It fails low by a factor of 1.8. The greediest legal base hold in the slice, 3 x docking_collar at 85 kg = 255 kg, still reaches only 1.28x. Dry mass cannot close it either: at the Researcher's 525 kg ballistic-coefficient floor, 97.4/525 = 1.19x.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Raise suborbital and low piece masses — the slice needs roughly 150 kg per crushed slot, so the sub-10 kg smalls (insulation_blanket_wad 4, antenna_boom_stub 6.5, crumpled_hull_panel 9) and the band's mediums must go up close to an order of magnitude; base_slots 6 and dry_mass_kg 900 are both fixed by the GDD and the baseline and must not move.\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"ablation_optimum_high_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The cheapest descent is 1 to 2 skims from the high band; no band's optimum exceeds the high band's; each minimum matches optimal_skims; and the curve is what the stated model actually produces.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"High curve [48.42, 43.61, 42.82, 45.68] has its minimum at index 2 — inside 1-2, i.e. a 3-pass descent, inside 2.3.1's 2-4 committed passes. Optima by band: suborbital 20.32 (idx 1) < low 29.88 (idx 1) < high 42.82 (idx 2), so plate cost rises with altitude. optimal_skims {1, 1, 2} matches the three minima exactly. Model recomputation, cost(k) = 0.53*sum_{i=0..k}1.5^i + 0.0005*(heat_index*mult[k])^2.4 + k*0.0005*32^2.4, with 32^2.4 = 4095.9 so the skim term is 2.048 per skim: low k=0 = 0.53 + 0.0005*100^2.4 (63095.7) + 0 = 32.08 (array 32.08); low k=2 = 2.5175 + 0.0005*89^2.4 (47702) + 4.096 = 30.46 (array 30.47); suborbital k=0 = 0.53 + 0.0005*83^2.4 (40345) = 20.70 (array 20.70); suborbital k=1 = 1.325 + 0.0005*77.19^2.4 (33895) + 2.048 = 20.32 (array 20.32); high k=1 = 1.325 + 0.0005*110.67^2.4 (80471) + 2.048 = 43.61 (array 43.61); high k=3 = 4.306 + 0.0005*104.72^2.4 (70481) + 6.144 = 45.69 (array 45.68). All eight recomputed points agree within 0.02 percentage points.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_multiplier_matches_measured\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The claimed skim_heat_multiplier matches what the simulator flew, high band, entry by entry.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Claimed [1, 0.93, 0.89, 0.88] against measured high-band skim_heat_multiplier_measured [1, 0.984, 0.968, 0.954], taken at fixed entry_depth 0. Differences 0.000, 0.054, 0.078, 0.074 — every index inside the 0.10 tolerance, worst case index 2 at 0.078. The error is one-signed (the guess overstates cooling at every index), so it sits just inside the gate rather than comfortably inside it.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_curve_saturates_and_toll_grows\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"skim_heat_multiplier starts at 1.0, is non-increasing and flattens by its last two entries; cycle_toll_growth exceeds 1.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"[1, 0.93, 0.89, 0.88]: first entry exactly 1.0; steps -0.070, -0.040, -0.010, all non-positive; the final step (0.010) is a quarter of the previous (0.040) and a seventh of the first, so the benefit saturates rather than keeping its slope. cycle_toll_growth = 1.5 > 1, so the toll is geometric — cycle 4 alone costs 0.53*1.5^3 = 1.789% against a fourth-cycle heat saving of 0.0005*(0.89^2.4 - 0.88^2.4)*heat_index^2.4, which is 0.64 points at the high band. The toll overtakes the saving, which is what bounds skim count.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_full_hold_descent_speed\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Parachute descent speed at full hold is under the 5 m/s soft-landing threshold but near enough to it that the Parachute upgrade is a real purchase (above about 3.5 m/s).\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"CdA = parachute_drag_coefficient 172.6 x reference_area_m2 3.5 = 604.1 m2. v = sqrt(2*m*g0/(rho0*CdA)) with g0 = 4.0 and rho0 = 0.6: empty 900 kg -> sqrt(7200/362.5) = 4.46 m/s; at the measured full hold 900+97.4 = 997.4 kg -> sqrt(7979/362.5) = 4.69 m/s; at the params' heaviest legal base hold 1044 kg -> sqrt(8352/362.5) = 4.80 m/s, which is the stated descent_speed_full_hold_ms. All three sit under 5.0 and above 3.5. The independent simulator, using its own inferred 6.0 m2 canopy, measures 3.39 empty / 3.57 full — also inside the window, at its bottom edge.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"tow_fee_clamps\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"The tow fee is zero inside the free radius, linear beyond it, clamps at exactly 50%, and can never go negative or exceed the cap.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"curve = 'linear', free_radius_m = 1000, max_fee_fraction = 0.5. fee(d) = 0.5*clamp((d-1000)/(628319-1000), 0, 1). At d = 1000 the numerator is 0, so fee = 0 and the clamp's lower bound holds for all d < 1000 (no negative branch). The far limit 628319 m equals pi*R = pi*200000 = 628318.5 m from the baseline radius, half the circumference, where the bracket is 1.0 and fee = 0.5 exactly. Midpoint check d = 314660 m -> (313660/627319) = 0.500 -> fee 0.250, linear. Upper clamp holds fee <= 0.5 for any greater d, so payout stays in [50%, 100%] of haul value and never debits savings.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"lazy_run_breaks_even\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Launch cost is at or below the value of three cheap suborbital pieces.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Cheapest suborbital piece is a solid small: size_class_base_value.small 40 x band_value_multiplier.suborbital 1.0 = 40. Three of them = 120. launch_cost 90 <= 120, i.e. 2.25 cheap pieces, inside 2.3.6's 'two to three'. Adding the optimum suborbital re-plating bill, 20.32% x replate_cost_per_pct 1.5 = 30.5, gives a total run cost of 120.5 against 120 for three pieces — dead even, and a six-small hold at 240 nets +119.5.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"no_shear_under_steady_tow\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Steady-state towing never shears a magnet: per-cable tension at the heaviest legal load stays below magnet_hold_force_n with margin.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"a = jetpack_thrust_n / (suit_mass_kg + towed). Heaviest legal two-piece load at base_tether_slots 2 is the two heaviest hand-tetherable pieces in the catalog, momentum_wheel_cluster 150 + instrument_bus_core 130 = 280 kg: a = 200/(120+280) = 0.500 m/s2, tension on the 150 kg cable = 75.0 N against magnet_hold_force_n 900 — a 12.0x margin. In-slice worst case, docking_collar 85 + propellant_tank_shell 72 = 157 kg: a = 200/277 = 0.722 m/s2, tension 61.4 N, 14.7x margin. The theoretical ceiling is jetpack_thrust_n itself (tension = m_piece*F/(m_suit+m_towed) < F = 200 N for any load), 4.5x below the hold force, so no legal steady-state tow can shear at any tether count.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"staging_is_one_way\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"No parameter anywhere reintroduces thrust after staging.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The only thrust fields in the params are flight.thrust_n 11000 and flight.rcs_thrust_n 500, both drawing on flight.fuel_capacity_kg via fuel_burn_kgs / rcs_fuel_burn_kgs — all four belong to the jettisoned stage, and there is no post-stage, descent-stage or reserve-thrust field of any kind. The only post-stage impulse in the value set is eva.jetpack_thrust_n 200 N applied to the hull: 200/997.4 kg = 0.20 m/s2, the get-out-and-push rescue 2.3.1 explicitly permits and three orders of magnitude short of the 11000 N that flew the ship up.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_never_crushed_and_premium\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile is never crushable at any compactor tier present in the params, and the fragile premium makes one intact fragile piece worth several solid pieces of the same class and band.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"cargo.compactor_tier = 1, which crushes small only; no tier above 1 exists in the params or the upgrade list (the twelve purchases contain no compactor), and no parameter grants a crush exemption or a fragile-crushable flag, so the 'never crushable' rule has nothing to contradict it. fragile_value_premium 4.0: cracked_viewport_glass = 40 x 1.0 x 4 = 160, exactly four solid suborbital smalls; cracked_solar_array = 90 x 2.5 x 4 = 900, four solid low-band mediums. Because it also pays 4 uncrushed slots against a solid medium's 2, taking it displaces 450 of solid haul to gain 900 — a 2.0x net, so it is worth taking without being automatic.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"oversized_rejects_hand_magnet\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Oversized junk rejects the hand magnet.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"catalog.size_classes.oversized.hand_tetherable = false, against small = true and medium = true. All three oversized entries (scorched_fairing_half 210 kg, ruptured_pressure_vessel 340 kg, upper_stage_skirt 520 kg) inherit it and are annotated crane-cable only, so nothing in the shipping slice can be hand-tethered that should not be.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"twelve_upgrade_purchases\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Twelve upgrade purchases: six parts, two tiers each, tier 2 costing more than tier 1 for the same part, and each tier improving the stat it names.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"12 entries, 6 distinct parts (fuel_tank, thruster, storage, heat_shield, parachute, hand_magnet), tiers 1 and 2 once each. Cost t2 > t1 in every pair: 900>400, 800>350, 1000>450, 1100>500, 700>300, 600>250 (total spend 7350). Stat progression against the base params: fuel_capacity_kg 620->820->1050; thrust_n 11000->14000->18000; base_slots 6->9->12; plate_capacity_pct 100->140->190; parachute_drag_coefficient 172.6->230->300 (higher CdA = slower descent, so an improvement); hand_magnet t1 raises magnet_range_screens 0.125->0.1875 and t2 raises magnet_hold_force_n 900->1500 — different stats, but each strictly improves the base value it names.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"slice_bands_populated\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band the shipping slice needs is reachable: the catalog populates suborbital and low with at least one fragile piece and at least two size classes each.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Suborbital: 9 types across three size classes (5 small, 3 medium, 1 oversized) with 2 fragile — cracked_viewport_glass (small, 7 kg) and dish_reflector_shard (medium, 13 kg). Low: 10 types across three size classes (3 small, 6 medium, 1 oversized) with 3 fragile — cracked_solar_array (medium, 26 kg), star_tracker_head (small, 11 kg), antenna_feed_horn (small, 9.5 kg). Both bands give the player a crushable small (compactor tier 1), an uncrushed medium, and an uncrushable fragile, so crushing and fragility are both met inside the slice.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"no_target_was_moved\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"No rule was satisfied by moving a quantity the design document fixes in prose.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Every GDD-stated constant is at its stated value: cargo.base_slots = 6 (2.2's 'Cargo reads 4 of 6' and the appendix's open question), eva.base_tether_slots = 2 (same appendix line), eva.magnet_range_screens = 0.125 (2.3.4's ~1/8 screen), landing.soft_landing_ms = 5, no_gear_multiplier = 2, fragile_multiplier = 2 (2.3.2), tow_fee.max_fee_fraction = 0.5 (2.3.3), reentry.heat_capacity = 100, heat_dissipation_s = 5, cargo_damage_interval_s = 3 (2.3.1), ablation.plate_capacity_pct = 100, cargo.compactor_tier = 1 (4.1's tier-1-only slice), flight.dry_mass_kg = 900 (the Researcher's baseline assumption). Notably the full-hold-mass rule was left failing at 1.11x rather than rescued by inflating base_slots or deflating dry_mass_kg, and optimal_skims {1,1,2} maps to 2/2/3 committed passes, inside 2.3.1's 2-4 rather than a widened target.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"observations\": [\n    \"The measured sweep reports reached=false, why='crashed on ascent', fuel_remaining_kg=0 for all three bands, while the params' own rocket equation closes comfortably: ve = 11000/4.6 = 2391 m/s, dv = 2391*ln(1520/900) = 1253 m/s against a ~950 m/s suborbital ascent, liftoff TWR = 11000/(1520*4.0) = 1.81. No check in my list covers powered ascent, so nothing here failed on it — but a value set whose simulator cannot get off the pad is not flyable whatever the descent numbers say, and this should be settled before any of the passing checks above are trusted.\",\n    \"The ablation curve passes every structural test and is faithfully reproduced from its own coefficients, yet the simulator measures cheapest_pass_count = 1 in all nine band x load descents, with ablation rising monotonically (high full hold 11.7 -> 17.6 -> 18.9 -> 19.9%). The params predict the high band saves 5.6 points by skimming twice; the flight measures it costing 5.9 points. The curve is right about itself and wrong about the game.\",\n    \"skim_heat_multiplier clears the high-band tolerance at 0.078 worst case, but the other two bands are not tested by that check and are worse: suborbital claims 0.88 at three skims where the simulator flew 0.684, a gap of 0.196. Adopting the measured arrays wholesale would reprice every entry in cost_curve, not just the high band's.\",\n    \"Measured touchdown is 3.39 m/s empty and 3.57 m/s at full hold — 29% below the 5 m/s hard-landing line, and only 5.3% of spread between an empty ship and a greedy one. Landing grade is therefore not a live mechanic anywhere in the slice, and the Parachute upgrade has nothing to counter. This is downstream of the full-hold mass failure, not independent of it.\",\n    \"The suborbital cost curve is nearly flat: 20.70 direct against 20.32 at one skim, a 1.8% saving. Whether to skim from the first band is arithmetically a non-decision, which is defensible as a forgiving tutorial band but means the player's first encounter with the game's signature mechanic teaches that it does not matter.\",\n    \"landing.parachute_area_m2 does not exist as a parameter; the canopy area is folded into parachute_drag_coefficient 172.6 (x reference_area_m2 3.5 = 604 m2) and the simulator had to infer 6.0 m2 from the descent-speed claim instead. Two artifacts are modelling the same physical part with numbers that differ by two orders of magnitude, and neither is wrong on its own terms.\",\n    \"Measured difficulty inverts with altitude at optimal play: at each band's cheapest descent the high band is the coolest return (peak heat 65, ablation 11.7%) and suborbital the hottest (69, 13.5%). The risk ordering the params assert (heat_index 83/100/119, plate cost 20/30/43) only appears at deep committed entries. Whether the return leg gets harder with altitude currently depends on the player choosing to fly it badly.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":182279,"ttft_stream_ms":1844,"time_to_request_ms":224,"type":"result","duration_ms":276917,"uuid":"934257bb-fb74-4a56-a5f4-bccc4b86392f"}
